# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The working tree is Windows format: text is checked out with CRLF, and stored
# in the repository with LF. So an end-of-line-only difference is never a
# content change - `git diff` stays empty and there is nothing to commit, no
# matter which side last wrote the file. This repository is edited from Windows
# and from WSL in one shared working tree, which is why that matters.
#
# One caveat, worth knowing rather than fighting: `git status` compares a file's
# size against what the index cached, so a file whose line endings were flipped
# shows up as ` M` even though `git diff` is empty and a commit would be a
# no-op. It clears once the file is written with the endings checkout produces,
# or with `rm <file> && git checkout -- <file>` after committing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=crlf

# .NET sources and project files - Windows format, stated explicitly rather
# than left to the catch-all above.
*.cs            text eol=crlf
*.csproj        text eol=crlf
*.props         text eol=crlf
*.targets       text eol=crlf
*.sln           text eol=crlf
*.slnx          text eol=crlf
*.config        text eol=crlf
*.json          text eol=crlf
.editorconfig   text eol=crlf

# Windows scripts.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Must keep LF even on Windows: a .sh with CRLF fails under WSL with a
# "bad interpreter" error that reads as a missing shell rather than as a
# line-ending problem.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# LightBurn project files are XML text, consumed by LightBurn on Windows.
# Golden comparison normalises line endings (GoldenCardTests.Normalise), so no
# test depends on them.
*.lbrn          text eol=crlf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.ezd           binary
*.ai            binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.dll           binary
*.exe           binary
*.snk           binary

# The one file LightBurn itself saved — evidence about the format, kept byte-exact.
# Never normalised, so what is in the repository is what LightBurn wrote.
plugin/skills/laser-lightburn/probe/07-fill-line.lbrn -text
